      "title": "Conceptualizing a Prompt Regression Test for Local LLM Development",
      "body": "This post discusses a hypothetical approach to prompt regression testing for local LLM experimentation. The intention is to create a structured method for evaluating prompt changes without any actual test runs or results yet. \n\nThe plan is to establish a baseline using a defined set of 12 fixed test prompts (F1). This set would include prompts designed to elicit direct answers and others that are intentionally ambiguous or lack sufficient data for a conclusive answer (F3). \n\nTo perform a hypothetical regression test, one would compare the output generated by an 'old' prompt against the output from a 'new' prompt, using the exact same model configuration and parameters for both (F2). This controlled comparison is key. \n\nIt's important to reiterate that this is a purely theoretical exercise; no performance data or improvements have been observed or measured (F4). The focus is on the design of the testing methodology itself. Discussion on potential pitfalls or refinements to this hypothetical testing structure is invited.",
